ion mindset and discipline.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(94,'HR','easy','HR Question 94: How do you approach presenting technical concepts to non-technical stakeholders in a collaborative software engineering environment?','When managing presenting technical concepts to non-technical stakeholders, my approach is grounded in professional responsibility and open communication. I avoid jargon, use concrete analogies, and focus on business outcomes and user impact rather than low-level implementation details.','Focus on empathy for the audience and clarity.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(95,'HR','medium','HR Question 95: How do you approach disagreeing with a team decision in a collaborative software engineering environment?','When managing disagreeing with a team decision, my approach is grounded in professional responsibility and open communication. I voice my perspective with objective data during the discussion phase. Once the team reaches a consensus, I commit fully to the agreed direction even if it differed from my initial preference.','Demonstrate the \'disagree and commit\' principle.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(96,'HR','hard','HR Question 96: How do you approach handling burnout risk in a collaborative software engineering environment?','When managing handling burnout risk, my approach is grounded in professional responsibility and open communication. I monitor my focus levels, enforce clear boundaries between work and rest, and communicate early with leads if workload volume begins compromising code quality.','Show proactive health and energy management.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(97,'HR','easy','HR Question 97: How do you approach ethical dilemmas in software development in a collaborative software engineering environment?','When managing ethical dilemmas in software development, my approach is grounded in professional responsibility and open communication. In any situation involving data privacy or security shortcuts, I advocate for user protection and transparent compliance with technical standards, escalating concerns professionally.','Emphasize integrity and user trust.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(98,'HR','medium','HR Question 98: How do you approach managing remote work effectively in a collaborative software engineering environment?','When managing managing remote work effectively, my approach is grounded in professional responsibility and open communication. Remote work requires clear asynchronous documentation, punctual participation in syncs, and proactive status updates to maintain team alignment.','Highlight written communication and self-discipline.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(99,'HR','hard','HR Question 99: How do you approach mentoring peers or juniors in a collaborative software engineering environment?','When managing mentoring peers or juniors, my approach is grounded in professional responsibility and open communication. I focus on guiding colleagues to discover solutions rather than simply giving answers, fostering their problem-solving independence and confidence.','Show empathy, patience, and structured guidance.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(100,'HR','easy','HR Question 100: How do you approach celebrating team success vs individual credit in a collaborative software engineering environment?','When managing celebrating team success vs individual credit, my approach is grounded in professional responsibility and open communication. Software is a team sport. I always ensure collective efforts are recognized and acknowledge peers who contributed to unblocking the project.','Show humility and team-first orientation.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(101,'Technical','medium','Explain the difference between var, let, and const in JavaScript.','var is function-scoped and hoisted with an initial value of undefined. let and const are block-scoped and hoisted without initialization, remaining in the Temporal Dead Zone until declared. const cannot be reassigned after declaration, while let can.','Mention scoping rules, hoisting behavior, temporal dead zone, and modern best practices (const by default).','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(102,'Technical','hard','How does the browser event loop work in JavaScript?','JavaScript is single-threaded. The event loop continuously monitors the Call Stack and task queues. When the Call Stack is empty, it processes microtasks (Promise callbacks, queueMicrotask) first before moving to macrotasks (setTimeout, setInterval, I/O events).','Explain Call Stack, Web APIs, Microtask Queue vs Macrotask Queue, and execution order.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(103,'Technical','medium','What are the differences between localStorage, sessionStorage, and cookies?','localStorage persists data across browser sessions with ~5-10MB storage. sessionStorage persists data only for the current tab lifetime. Cookies are small (~4KB) data stores sent with every HTTP request, supporting server-side flags like HttpOnly and Secure.','Compare storage capacity, lifespan, network transmission, and security flags.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(104,'Technical','easy','Explain CSS Box Model and the difference between content-box and border-box.','The CSS Box Model consists of content, padding, border, and margin. In content-box, width applies only to content, so padding and border add to the total element width. In border-box, width includes content, padding, and border, making responsive sizing much more intuitive.','Draw or describe the 4 layers and explain why box-sizing: border-box is standard in modern resets.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(105,'Technical','easy','What is the difference between synchronous and asynchronous code in JavaScript?','Synchronous code executes sequentially, blocking subsequent execution until the current operation finishes. Asynchronous code executes without blocking the main thread, utilizing callbacks, Promises, or async/await to handle deferred results via the event loop.','Give examples: blocking file/loop operations vs non-blocking fetch/timer calls.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(106,'Technical','medium','What are RESTful APIs and what are their primary HTTP methods?','REST (Representational State Transfer) is an architectural style for networked applications. Primary HTTP methods include GET (retrieve resource), POST (create resource), PUT (replace resource), PATCH (partial update), and DELETE (remove resource).','Explain idempotency, statelessness, and standard HTTP response status code conventions.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(107,'Technical','medium','What is the difference between SQL and NoSQL databases?','SQL databases (MySQL, PostgreSQL) are relational, use structured schemas with tables, enforce ACID properties, and excel at complex joins. NoSQL databases (MongoDB, Redis) are non-relational, flexible schema-less stores (document, key-value), and scale horizontally with ease.','Compare schema structure, ACID vs BASE consistency, scaling mechanisms, and use cases.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(108,'Technical','hard','What is Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) and how is it handled?','CORS is a browser security mechanism using HTTP headers to permit or restrict resources requested from a different origin (domain, protocol, or port). The server responds with Access-Control-Allow-Origin headers and handles preflight OPTIONS requests for non-simple requests.','Explain same-origin policy, preflight requests, and server-side configuration headers.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(109,'Technical','easy','What is the difference between == and === in JavaScript?','== is the loose equality operator that performs type coercion before comparison. === is the strict equality operator that compares both value and type without coercion, returning false if types differ.','Give classic examples like \'5\' == 5 (true) vs \'5\' === 5 (false).','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(110,'Technical','hard','Explain closures in JavaScript with a practical use case.','A closure is a function bundled together with references to its surrounding lexical scope. Even when executed outside its lexical environment, it retains access to variables in that scope. Practical use cases include data privacy, factory functions, and memoization.','Demonstrate a counter or private variable pattern.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(111,'Technical','easy','What is Responsive Web Design and how are media queries used?','Responsive Web Design ensures web layouts adapt gracefully to diverse screen sizes. It uses fluid grid layouts, flexible images, and CSS media queries (@media) that apply styling rules based on viewport width, orientation, or resolution.','Mention viewport meta tag, mobile-first approach, and CSS flexbox/grid.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(112,'Technical','medium','What is the difference between flexbox and CSS Grid?','Flexbox is primarily a one-dimensional layout system (row OR column), ideal for distributing space among items in an interface component. CSS Grid is a two-dimensional layout system (rows AND columns), ideal for architecting overall page layout structures.','Contrast 1D vs 2D layout models with concrete examples.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(113,'Technical','hard','What is SQL Injection and how do prepared statements prevent it?','SQL Injection occurs when malicious user input is concatenated directly into a database query string, altering the query logic. Prepared statements prevent this by separating the query structure from user data; inputs are parameterized and treated strictly as literal values, never executable SQL.','Contrast string concatenation with PDO parameterized binding.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(114,'Technical','medium','What are Promises and how does async/await improve asynchronous code?','A Promise represents the eventual completion or failure of an asynchronous operation (pending, fulfilled, rejected). async/await is syntactic sugar over Promises, allowing asynchronous code to be written and read like synchronous code, improving readability and error handling with try/catch.','Discuss Promise chaining vs try/catch readability with async/await.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(115,'Technical','hard','What is indexing in a database and how does it improve query performance?','A database index is a data structure (typically B-Tree or Hash) that allows the database engine to locate records quickly without scanning the entire table. While indexing accelerates SELECT queries dramatically, it incurs overhead on INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE operations.','Explain B-Tree mechanics, search efficiency (O(log n)), and write trade-offs.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(116,'Technical','easy','Technical Question 16: Explain the core architecture and practical implementation of session management vs JWT authentication.','In modern web systems, session management vs JWT authentication is essential for stability and scale. Session-based auth stores session data server-side (in memory or database) with a session ID cookie. JWT stores signed claims inside a token client-side, enabling stateless authentication across distributed microservices.','Contrast server memory overhead with token invalidation challenges.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(117,'Technical','medium','Technical Question 17: Explain the core architecture and practical implementation of HTTP/1.1 vs HTTP/2 and multiplexing.','In modern web systems, HTTP/1.1 vs HTTP/2 and multiplexing is essential for stability and scale. HTTP/1.1 suffers from head-of-line blocking and opens multiple TCP connections. HTTP/2 introduces binary framing, request multiplexing over a single TCP connection, header compression (HPACK), and server push.','Focus on multiplexing and single TCP connection benefits.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(118,'Technical','hard','Technical Question 18: Explain the core architecture and practical implementation of web caching strategies and Cache-Control headers.','In modern web systems, web caching strategies and Cache-Control headers is essential for stability and scale. Web caching reduces latency and server load. The Cache-Control header directives (max-age, no-cache, no-store, immutable) combined with ETags govern browser and CDN caching lifetimes.','Explain max-age, ETag validation, and CDN edge caching.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(119,'Technical','easy','Technical Question 19: Explain the core architecture and practical implementation of WebSocket protocol vs long polling.','In modern web systems, WebSocket protocol vs long polling is essential for stability and scale. WebSockets provide persistent, full-duplex bidirectional communication over a single TCP connection with minimal overhead. Long polling repeatedly opens HTTP requests, incurring substantial header latency.','Contrast bidirectional full-duplex sockets with unidirectional HTTP polling.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(120,'Technical','medium','Technical Question 20: Explain the core architecture and practical implementation of frontend performance optimization techniques.','In modern web systems, frontend performance optimization techniques is essential for stability and scale. Optimizations include code splitting, lazy loading images and components, minifying assets, tree shaking, critical CSS inlining, and optimizing Core Web Vitals (LCP, FID, CLS).','Highlight measurable performance metrics and asset bundling techniques.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(121,'Technical','hard','Technical Question 21: Explain the core architecture and practical implementation of semantic HTML and accessibility standards.','In modern web systems, semantic HTML and accessibility standards is essential for stability and scale. Semantic HTML elements (header, nav, main, article) provide structural meaning to browsers and assistive technologies like screen readers, improving SEO and compliance with WCAG guidelines.','Mention accessibility, screen readers, and SEO.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(122,'Technical','easy','Technical Question 22: Explain the core architecture and practical implementation of CSS specificity and the cascade algorithm.','In modern web systems, CSS specificity and the cascade algorithm is essential for stability and scale. CSS specificity determines which rule takes precedence when multiple selectors target an element. It is calculated based on inline styles (1000), IDs (100), classes/attributes/pseudo-classes (10), and elements/pseudo-elements (1).','Explain the specificity calculation order.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(123,'Technical','medium','Technical Question 23: Explain the core architecture and practical implementation of Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) prevention.','In modern web systems, Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) prevention is essential for stability and scale. XSS occurs when malicious scripts are injected into trusted websites. Prevention requires sanitizing and escaping user input, implementing Content Security Policy (CSP), and avoiding unsafe APIs like innerHTML.','Distinguish between stored, reflected, and DOM-based XSS.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(124,'Technical','hard','Technical Question 24: Explain the core architecture and practical implementation of Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) tokens.','In modern web systems, Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) tokens is essential for stability and scale. CSRF tricks an authenticated user into executing unwanted actions. It is prevented using anti-CSRF synchronizer tokens validated on state-changing requests and using SameSite cookie attributes.','Explain the mechanism of forged requests and token verification.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(125,'Technical','easy','Technical Question 25: Explain the core architecture and practical implementation of database normalization and normal forms.','In modern web systems, database normalization and normal forms is essential for stability and scale. Database normalization organizes tables to reduce data redundancy and eliminate anomalies. 1NF requires atomic values, 2NF eliminates partial dependencies, and 3NF eliminates transitive dependencies.','Explain 1NF, 2NF, and 3NF simply.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(126,'Technical','medium','Technical Question 26: Explain the core architecture and practical implementation of session management vs JWT authentication.','In modern web systems, session management vs JWT authentication is essential for stability and scale. Session-based auth stores session data server-side (in memory or database) with a session ID cookie. JWT stores signed claims inside a token client-side, enabling stateless authentication across distributed microservices.','Contrast server memory overhead with token invalidation challenges.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(127,'Technical','hard','Technical Question 27: Explain the core architecture and practical implementation of HTTP/1.1 vs HTTP/2 and multiplexing.','In modern web systems, HTTP/1.1 vs HTTP/2 and multiplexing is essential for stability and scale. HTTP/1.1 suffers from head-of-line blocking and opens multiple TCP connections. HTTP/2 introduces binary framing, request multiplexing over a single TCP connection, header compression (HPACK), and server push.','Focus on multiplexing and single TCP connection benefits.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(128,'Technical','easy','Technical Question 28: Explain the core architecture and practical implementation of web caching strategies and Cache-Control headers.','In modern web systems, web caching strategies and Cache-Control headers is essential for stability and scale. Web caching reduces latency and server load. The Cache-Control header directives (max-age, no-cache, no-store, immutable) combined with ETags govern browser and CDN caching lifetimes.','Explain max-age, ETag validation, and CDN edge caching.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(129,'Technical','medium','Technical Question 29: Explain the core architecture and practical implementation of WebSocket protocol vs long polling.','In modern web systems, WebSocket protocol vs long polling is essential for stability and scale. WebSockets provide persistent, full-duplex bidirectional communication over a single TCP connection with minimal overhead. Long polling repeatedly opens HTTP requests, incurring substantial header latency.','Contrast bidirectional full-duplex sockets with unidirectional HTTP polling.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(130,'Technical','hard','Technical Question 30: Explain the core architecture and practical implementation of frontend performance optimization techniques.','In modern web systems, frontend performance optimization techniques is essential for stability and scale. Optimizations include code splitting, lazy loading images and components, minifying assets, tree shaking, critical CSS inlining, and optimizing Core Web Vitals (LCP, FID, CLS).','Highlight measurable performance metrics and asset bundling techniques.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(131,'Technical','easy','Technical Question 31: Explain the core architecture and practical implementation of semantic HTML and accessibility standards.','In modern web systems, semantic HTML and accessibility standards is essential for stability and scale. Semantic HTML elements (header, nav, main, article) provide structural meaning to browsers and assistive technologies like screen readers, improving SEO and compliance with WCAG guidelines.','Mention accessibility, screen readers, and SEO.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(132,'Technical','medium','Technical Question 32: Explain the core architecture and practical implementation of CSS specificity and the cascade algorithm.','In modern web systems, CSS specificity and the cascade algorithm is essential for stability and scale. CSS specificity determines which rule takes precedence when multiple selectors target an element. It is calculated based on inline styles (1000), IDs (100), classes/attributes/pseudo-classes (10), and elements/pseudo-elements (1).','Explain the specificity calculation order.','2026-10-05 06:33:25'),(133,'Technical','hard','Technical Question 33: Explain the core architecture and practical implementation of Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) prevention.','In modern web systems, Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) prevention is essential for stability and 